ir counterparts in New  York City and then were blamed for  having cholera saying it was spread  either by beer or occasionally by  communion wine that one was interesting  and then sort of as infectious disease  has spread this time we've seen a lot of  people talking about New Orleans and you  know Mardi Gras being the spot that  spread it through New Orleans because  those people needed to party which is  not really totally fair to New Orleans  and it's it's still very present I think  in the ways that we talk about kovat and  the ways that we have talked about  disease spread now and really for the  past several thousand years  unfortunately the idea not just of the  disease as punishment for sin but of  Prayer maybe as a protector how far back  does that go I mean we recently saw this  video of a woman leaving a mega church  where she said she's covered in the  blood of Jesus so she's fine  and so on and so forth that I'm guessing  that's also not new know also not new  very much not new it's what's  interesting is that it it the ways that  that worked kind of pre-reformation are  a little bit different and it was more  kind of prayer covering the whole  community but also a sort of acceptance  that disease was part of life now and in  particular in the United States there  have been fights about national prayer  too  epidemics all the way back to the 18th  century right in 1721 the there was a  large smallpox outbreak in Boston and  there are fights about prayer and  predestination as they begin to  introduce inoculation and whether you  should simply pray that you survive  because if you're a good person you'll  survive or whether you should take this  inoculation which at that moment was  viewed as possibly you know violating  predestination by messing with the plan  and then in the 1830s and 1850s with  cholera on the east coast of the United  States there are some impressively  intense fights about national prayer to  prevent the epidemic that in some cases  actually threatened to unseat a variety  of politicians and turn into political  issues as well as religious issues so  that one's old and very embedded in  American culture so for me this is a  part of at least right now seeing the  current administration's handling of the  virus at least over the last month  really as a cultural war rather than a  medical war and I did a segment about  this earlier this week my perspective is  that they've essentially failed on the  medical war so they're making it about  culture and values which are much more  pliable in terms of empirically  determining what it is that's going on  and so masks have become a signal of  your perspective as to what's going on  open versus closed has become an issue  of government tyranny and so on and so  forth is this the way this often goes  where it becomes a culture war hmm  you know honestly having studied a lot  of disease history I would say that this  kind of polarization of responding to  disease is fairly unusual  the fights so the fights around  infectious disease do often fall on  cultural lines right  so there's there's an author I love  called de certeau who talks about the  ways that in that in certain moments the  things that underlie society the  detentions that are already there kind  of break apart and come to light he  talks about these tensions that exist  that we all live inside sort of rising  up from under the streets it's a  beautiful passage but I think the  commonality among infectious disease  outbreaks is that they intensify and  make very visible social decision social  divisions social tensions and existing  problems but rarely do you see this kind  of pretending that a disease just  doesn't exist in communities where it  does it's I obviously am studying this  as I'm working on it  because it's both fascinating and a  little disturbing - what do you  attribute it I mean why it seems too  simplistic to say well it's because of  Trump I mean he seems to be the one who  is at the top of the federal government  right now but there's some element of  society allowing that to take place as  well right absolutely I think that  America for a long time has been deeply  divided along urban and rural lines and  that has very much played out in kovat  where the early outbreaks were very much  in urban areas where people travel more  and it's only now that they're really  moving into rural areas so for a long  time the pattern of kovat in the country  actually exemplified that division  I think the America has for a long time  had some intense class division among  types of workers and that is also very  much playing out here where a lot of the  a lot of workers some  can't stay home and so for those who can  responding to the disease is a different  type of ask right asking me to work from  home on history historical stuff or  policy is a very different ask than  asking that of someone who works in a  factory we're working from home isn't an  option so some of it I think is also  about the ways people have to justify  their own need to you know eat and  continue working and feed their families  and then some of it is the intense  polarization that we've experienced over  really the past ten years I think the  divisions in what news we watch what  radio shows we listen to you what  newspapers we read have really made it  possible for there to be multiple worlds  based on the same information in a way  that just hasn't historically always  been true when it comes to talking now  about stigma of disease itself there's  certain diseases that have more social  stigma attached to them so certainly  sexually transmitted infections and  diseases would be one category within  cancer lung cancer sometimes generates  less sympathy because it can be  connected to the bad act of smoking  cigarettes as an example where does the  where do these types of pandemics that  like coronavirus kind of fit into the  stigma analysis so I wish I wish dearly  that I could tell you that pandemic  disease was an exemption from stigma  unfortunately I could I don't get to say  that so it's lower on the scale than  things like STDs or lung cancer higher  on the scale than zero so you're  starting to see problems where people  have recovered from Kovan and are having  difficulty still getting ambulances to  to their house or their neighbors don't  want them to leave the house or want  them to move out if the neighborhood's  businesses where the owner has recovered  where no one will go inside because  they're afraid of the virus and I think  that that is something we will probably  see expanding there has also  unfortunately been a lot of  racialization of this virus that has  caused serious problems in a lot of  Chinese American communities but also  unfortunately other Asian American  communities that isn't so much disease  stigma as disease blame and kind of  racialization of the disease so I think  that those are those are things that we  as a society and as a nation need to  consciously combat and pay attention to  you because know someone that were  covered from kovat four months ago is  not going to give you the virus but  people are still afraid it's one of the  ways where the stigma is entering a  little more is with some of the reports  that for example of the younger people  that are dying overwhelmingly they are  obese and obesity is often associated  with you know moral failings rather than  medical conditions so it seems maybe  that's one way in which it's creeping in  lately oh yeah  I actually started my the first year and  a half I was in public health I was  doing research on obesity stigma I found  infectious disease to be a less  depressing topic and tells you think  about how bad it is yeah I think the  absolutely a lot of the things about  pre-existing conditions are allowing  people to blame those who've died of  kovat for their own deaths which is  deeply frustrating you know most of the  most of the pre-existing conditions that  really predispose people to dying from  kovin are those that are really  frequently linked to structural factors  in economics in race in  access to medical care in location and  so it is kind of quadruply frustrating  to then watch them become a way to  write-off covet deaths because they're  almost entirely structurally linked  comorbidities I guess this is the best  way to put it  yeah no question about that and that's  often missing from you know the science  science can tell us a lot of the facts  but it doesn't it rarely tells us about  the economic and sociological factors  that kind of led to those being being  the facts we've been speaking with  animal Doon who's a former science  policy advisor at the Department of  Health and Human Services Anna so great  to speak to you I really appreciate your  time course

